The LUKS header is the part of an encrypted volume that holds all information
regarding encryption. This includes information about what secrets have been
enrolled and can be used for unlocking. It is a tiny portion of the partition
that, if corrupted, will render the entire file system unusable and all data on
it lost. For this reason, it is advisable to create a backup of the header and
put it in a safe location. Should the original header get corrupted, you can
restore this header with the following command:
cryptsetup luksHeaderRestore --header-backup-file=<backup-file> <LUKS-partition>

Creating a backup of the LUKS header or restoring the backup does not alter the
volumes encryption setup (before corruption) in any way. After restoring the
header, you will still need access to your enrolled secrets to unlock the
encrypted partition.
